Automatic Semantic Layer Generation
The demo showed how an existing Power BI report (.pbix) can be imported into Snowflake Cortex Analyst UI. Snowflake parses the Power BI semantic model (measures, dimensions, relationships) and automatically generates a Snowflake Semantic View, eliminating the need to manually rebuild business logic in Snowflake.

Centralized Metric Definitions inside Snowflake
The generated Semantic View stores business metrics, dimensions, and table relationships directly in Snowflake. This allows teams to standardize KPI definitions at the data platform layer rather than duplicating logic across BI dashboards or ad-hoc SQL queries.

Natural Language Analytics using Cortex Analyst
Once the semantic view is created, Cortex Analyst enables natural language queries against the data model. User questions are interpreted by the LLM, translated into SQL using the semantic model context, executed on Snowflake, and returned as structured results.

Reduced SQL Dependency for Business Users
Because Cortex Analyst understands the semantic structure of the data, analysts or stakeholders can retrieve insights using business language instead of writing SQL, while still relying on governed Snowflake datasets.

Reuse of Existing BI Investments
Organizations with existing Power BI semantic models can quickly onboard them into Snowflake without redesigning the entire model. This helps accelerate adoption of AI-driven analytics workflows.

AI-Ready Data Platform Architecture
By placing the semantic layer directly inside Snowflake, the platform becomes compatible with LLM-based analytics interfaces, enabling conversational querying while maintaining data governance, RBAC security, and warehouse execution performance.

